#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qb-bump — carry a landed harness change onto this machine, as far as sudo allows (#267).

  qb-bump                       pull; if stale, prepare the flake bump, BUILD it, propose it
  qb-bump --json                the same answer as a document
  qb-bump --apply               the WHOLE job, run by a PERSON: pull, bump, build, switch
  qb-bump --apply --dry-run     all of that except the switch, whose command is printed
  qb-bump --apply --cached      switch onto the proposal already in the cache, prepare nothing
  qb-bump --no-pull             compare against the two trees exactly as they stand

  0  nothing to carry — the harness on PATH is this checkout's
  1  cannot tell — not in a quarterback checkout, a harness row that is neither
     ok nor fail, no qb-doctor, no nix, no consuming flake, or more than one
  2  a bump is prepared and BUILT, and one command by a person finishes it
  3  nothing to apply — no prepared bump, a moved tree, no terminal, or a bad flag
  4  the bump does not build — refused to propose it

**Landing is not deploying.** Sixteen releases landed on 2026-08-22 and the harness
half of every one of them reached this box only when a person remembered to run
`nix flake update` by hand. The pin was 162 commits behind that morning — eleven
of the repo's binaries were simply absent from PATH, including `qb-reconcile`,
whose systemd units had therefore never been installable — and it was stale again
by 14:00, and again by 22:00. Nothing in the fleet carries a merged change to a
machine, which is #267, and this is the half of it that ACTS.

**It is not a second detector.** `qb-doctor`'s `harness` row (#204) already
answers "is the harness on PATH the one this checkout has", and it has been
answering it correctly all day. This shells out to it — `qb-doctor --json --only
harness` — and reads the verdict. A comparison re-implemented here would be a
second opinion about a fact that already has one, and the two would disagree on
the day one of them learned something (`_same_after_packaging` and its wrapper
handling is exactly the kind of thing only one of two copies would ever get).

**The ceiling is sudo, and it is designed around rather than fought.** A
`nixos-rebuild switch` needs root. An agent has no root and should not go looking
for it, so this stops one step short: it prepares the change, builds it, proves it
evaluates, and hands a person a single command. That is the ten minutes; the
`sudo` is the ten seconds.

**A proposal that has not been built is a proposal to break somebody's machine.**
The first bump on 2026-08-22 failed on a collision — quarterback's module had
started installing `~/.claude/quarterback-workflow.md` while the consuming flake
still declared its own copy, and two definitions of one `home.file` path is an
eval error, not a merge. So the build is not a nicety here: a bump that does not
build is REFUSED and announced as a refusal, with the error, rather than proposed.

**It never commits in the consumer, and never reads its uncommitted work.** The
preparation happens on `git archive HEAD` unpacked into a temporary directory —
so a consumer's dirty working tree can neither be swept into what is built nor
have anything written over it. The only file this ever writes into a consumer's
checkout is `flake.lock`, only under `--apply`, and only when the human running it
is the one who typed the command. Committing that lock is the consumer's business
and stays theirs.

**"I cannot tell" is not "there is nothing to carry", and the difference is the
whole point of the tool.** This resolved the checkout it compares against from the
working directory and never said so, so run from a consumer's checkout it found no
`harness/` to compare, `qb-doctor` fell back to the tree beside itself — which for
an installed `qb-doctor` is the installed harness, always identical to itself — and
this printed "nothing to carry" about a box 74 commits and eleven releases behind.
That is a positive assertion of health, and a person who reads one stops looking.
So the checkout is resolved deliberately now (`--repo`, the working directory,
`QUARTERBACK_REPO`), it is NAMED in every report including the no-op, and no
checkout at all is `unknown`/1 rather than `current`/0 — see `resolve_repo` (#414).

**The consuming flake is a third thing, and it is found rather than assumed.**
`qb-doctor` records that it cannot find it, which is why its comparison is a
content proxy and not a pin comparison: it knows the checkout and the store path,
and the flake that CONSUMES the harness lives somewhere else on some hosts and
nowhere at all on others. So this asks four questions in order — `--flake`, the
environment, the site config, and a bounded scan for a `flake.lock` that pins this
repo — and refuses rather than guessing when the scan finds two. The third of
those is the door a consumer declares itself through
(`programs.quarterback-harness.consumer.flake`); the fourth is what runs on a
fleet that has declared nothing, which is every fleet today.

**A comparison against a stale checkout is the false all-clear wearing a different
hat.** The drift verdict answers "is the harness on PATH the one THIS CHECKOUT
has", so a checkout twenty commits behind origin agrees with an installed harness
twenty commits behind, and the answer comes back `current`/0 — "nothing to carry"
— about a box that is nothing of the sort. #414 closed this hole for a checkout
that was the wrong DIRECTORY; #533 closes it for a checkout that is the right
directory at the wrong COMMIT. So both trees are brought up to date before
anything is compared: the quarterback checkout, because it is what the comparison
is against, and the consuming flake, because its HEAD is what gets built.

**The pull is a fast-forward and is never allowed to become anything else.**
`git fetch`, then `git merge --ff-only`. A tree carrying a local commit or a
conflicting edit is REPORTED, not merged, rebased or reset — nothing here is
entitled to resolve somebody's divergence, and the commands that would
(`pull --rebase`, `reset --hard`, `stash`) are the ones that have eaten
uncommitted work on this fleet five times. A tree with no upstream is not a
failure; there is simply nothing to pull it up to, and that is said and stepped
over. A fetch that FAILS or a fast-forward that is REFUSED is a different thing:
there is a remote and this could not get level with it, so a `current` verdict
downgrades to `unknown`/1. Not knowing is the honest answer and it is the entire
reason #414 exists.

**Having pulled the consumer, it acts on the consumer.** A `nix-fleet` commit that
landed from another box is a rebuild this machine owes even when the harness pin
has not moved an inch. Pulling that in and then printing "nothing to carry" would
leave the person with a silently-changed checkout and no follow-through, which is
worse than never having pulled it. So a consumer that MOVED on the pull is enough
to prepare and switch on its own, and the report says which of the two reasons it
is acting on.

**The switch goes through the host's `rebuild` wrapper, because `nixos-rebuild
switch` lies.** It prints "Done. The new configuration is …" even when
`home-manager-<user>.service` has failed — so `home.file` links, user units and
dotfiles silently do not apply, which for this tool means the harness scripts it
exists to deliver do not arrive and the switch reports that they did. That is not
a hypothetical failure elsewhere in the system; it is precisely #267's failure,
reached by a different road. This fleet's `rebuild` exists to surface it. It is
not reimplemented here — that is the second-detector mistake this file already
refuses to make about drift — it is CALLED. Anything short of confident falls back
to the explicit `sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake` this file resolved itself,
which is always correct and merely misses the guard.

**What the wrapper check actually establishes, said plainly, because it is less
than it looks.** The wrapper's TEXT is read — never run — and used only when it
names exactly one flake directory and that directory is this one. That is a
heuristic and not a proof: a regex is not a shell parser, so whole-line comments
are dropped (a commented-out `flake=` above a live `--flake "path:$other#rescue"`
was the decoy that made that necessary) but a target assembled from variables can
still hide from it. The ATTRIBUTE is not read at all and cannot be — it never
appears in the text, because a wrapper derives it from the live hostname. That is
exactly why a MATCHED attribute is required: `resolve_attr` matches this machine's
hostName too, so the two agree by construction, while `--host laptop` on a desktop
agrees only by luck and is refused. Three things therefore bound this: the
flakeref must be this one and the only one, `--host` refuses the wrapper outright,
and `--no-wrapper` turns the path off.

**One command, because two is where the job used to stop.** `--apply` refused
whenever the cached proposal had gone stale, which is the state it is in most
times a person reaches for it — the agent prepared it an hour and three merges
ago. So `--apply` now runs the whole preparation itself and switches onto what it
has just proven rather than onto what somebody proved earlier; nix's own caching
makes re-proving an unchanged build a matter of seconds. `--cached` is the door
back to the old behaviour, for a host that has lost its network between the
preparation and the person. **The agent path is unchanged and still stops at the
password**: `qb-bump` with no flags never runs `sudo`, and the test that says so
is the design ceiling, not an assertion about the current code.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import io
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tarfile
import tempfile
import time
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# constants
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

#: How long `nix flake update` gets. It is one input against a network that may
#: be slow, and a minute of a hanging GitHub is not worth an hour of a hung timer.
UPDATE_TIMEOUT = 300

#: How long the system build gets. Generous on purpose: the first build after a
#: nixpkgs-touching bump compiles, and a build killed at ten minutes reports "does
#: not build" about a machine that builds fine — the one lie this file must not
#: tell, since a false refusal trains a reader to ignore the true one.
BUILD_TIMEOUT = 3600

#: How long each `git fetch` gets. Short on purpose: a fetch that cannot finish in
#: two minutes is a network problem, and the honest answer to a network problem is
#: "this tree could not be brought up to date" rather than a run that hangs.
FETCH_TIMEOUT = 120

#: How much of a failed build's output is carried into the report and the board
#: post. The tail, not the head: nix prints the trace last and the "these N
#: derivations will be built" list first.
ERROR_LINES = 40

#: Where a prepared proposal lives between the agent that built it and the person
#: who applies it. Under the cache dir because losing it costs a rerun of
#: something reproducible, never a decision.
CACHE = Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CACHE_HOME") or Path.home() / ".cache") \
    / "quarterback" / "harness-bump"

#: Roots the consumer scan looks in, one level deep, when nothing declares a
#: flake. `~/source` first because that is where this fleet keeps checkouts;
#: `~` because a single-repo host often keeps it there. Override with
#: `QUARTERBACK_CONSUMER_ROOTS` (colon-separated), which is also how a host with
#: its flake at `/etc/nixos` says so.
DEFAULT_ROOTS = ("~/source", "~")

#: Config keys, read from `~/.config/quarterback/config` — the same file `qb-env`
#: writes and `qb-doctor` parses, so a consumer that declares itself does it once.
FLAKE_KEY = "QUARTERBACK_CONSUMER_FLAKE"
ATTR_KEY = "QUARTERBACK_CONSUMER_ATTR"

#: The site key naming this host's `nixos-rebuild` wrapper, for a fleet whose
#: wrapper is not called `rebuild` or cannot be read. Declaring it is consent: a
#: declared command is used without the target check below, because somebody has
#: said what it builds and this file is not going to argue with them.
REBUILD_KEY = "QUARTERBACK_REBUILD_CMD"

#: The needs-human class this escalates under. `environment` from #279's
#: vocabulary: the decision is not a matter of taste or design, it is that this
#: machine's state can only be changed by somebody who can become root.
NEEDS_HUMAN_CLASS = "environment"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# small shared plumbing
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def run(argv: list[str], cwd: str | None = None, timeout: int = 60,
        env: dict | None = None) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """``(rc, stdout, stderr)``, with every failure mode as a return rather than a raise.

    A missing binary is ``(127, "", …)`` and a timeout is ``(124, "", …)``, both
    spelled the way a shell would spell them, because every caller here has to
    turn "could not run it" into a row rather than a traceback.
    """
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout, env=env)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return 127, "", f"{argv[0]}: not found"
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", f"{argv[0]}: timed out after {timeout}s"
    return p.returncode, p.stdout.strip(), p.stderr.strip()


def nix(*args: str, timeout: int = 60, cwd: str | None = None) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """`nix` with the two experimental features this file needs, asked for explicitly.

    `--extra-experimental-features` rather than relying on the host's `nix.conf`:
    this runs on machines the fleet does not configure, and "error: experimental
    Nix feature 'nix-command' is disabled" is a refusal that has nothing to do
    with the question being asked.
    """
    return run(["nix", "--extra-experimental-features", "nix-command flakes", *args],
               timeout=timeout, cwd=cwd)


def have_nix() -> bool:
    """Is there a nix on this host at all? A named function so a suite can say no.

    Asked once, in `main`, rather than discovered as a puzzling failure three
    steps in: a machine with no nix cannot take a flake bump by any route, and
    "no nixosConfigurations this can read" would be a true sentence about the
    wrong problem.
    """
    return shutil.which("nix") is not None


def site_config(path: Path | None = None) -> dict[str, str]:
    """`~/.config/quarterback/config`, parsed as the shell assignments it is.

    Parsed and never sourced, for the reason `qb-doctor.load_site_config` gives:
    the file is machine-generated on some hosts and hand-written on others, and a
    tool that prepares a proposal must not be the thing that executes an
    unreviewed line. This is the second implementation of that parser and it is
    deliberately kept to the same shape —
    `harness/tests/test_qb_bump.py::test_the_config_parser_agrees_with_qb_doctors`
    parses one fixture with both and fails if they ever disagree, which is the
    guard `test_in_flight_drift.py` puts between `qb-admit` and `harness_rules`.
    A bin script cannot import a loops module, and importing `qb-doctor` to read
    two keys would make every run of this depend on that file executing cleanly.
    """
    path = path or Path(os.environ.get(
        "QUARTERBACK_CONFIG",
        Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", Path.home() / ".config")) / "quarterback/config"))
    out: dict[str, str] = {}
    try:
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return out
    for line in text.splitlines():
        m = re.match(r"^\s*(?:export\s+)?([A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*)=(.*)$", line)
        if not m:
            continue
        value = m.group(2).strip()
        if len(value) >= 2 and value[0] == value[-1] and value[0] in "\"'":
            value = value[1:-1]
        out[m.group(1)] = value
    return out


def as_path(value: str | None) -> Path | None:
    """A config value naming a directory, expanded the way the shell that wrote it meant.

    `$HOME` and `~` both: the generated config on this fleet writes
    `QUARTERBACK_REPO="$HOME/source/quarterback"`, and reading that literally is
    how `qb-doctor` once reported confidently about a path with a dollar sign in
    it. `expandvars` substitutes; it does not execute.
    """
    if not value:
        return None
    return Path(os.path.expanduser(os.path.expandvars(value)))


def sha(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def tail(text: str, lines: int = ERROR_LINES) -> str:
    kept = [ln for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    return "\n".join(kept[-lines:])


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 0. git — both trees brought up to date, by fast-forward and by no other means
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

@dataclass
class Pulled:
    """What happened when one tree was asked to catch up with its upstream."""

    path: str
    upstream: str = ""
    before: str = ""
    after: str = ""
    #: Empty when the tree is level with its upstream. Otherwise, why it is not.
    why: str = ""
    #: True when there WAS an upstream and this could not get level with it — a
    #: failed fetch, a refused fast-forward. False when `why` merely records that
    #: there is nothing to pull from, which is not doubt about anything: a tree
    #: with no upstream cannot be behind one.
    blocked: bool = False

    @property
    def moved(self) -> bool:
        return bool(self.before) and bool(self.after) and self.before != self.after


def fast_forward(where: Path, timeout: int = FETCH_TIMEOUT) -> Pulled:
    """Fetch `where`'s upstream and fast-forward onto it. Never merge, rebase or reset.

    **The whole tool rests on a comparison, and a comparison against a stale tree
    is worth nothing.** `qb-doctor` answers "is the harness on PATH the one this
    checkout has"; run against a checkout that is itself behind origin, that
    question has a true answer and the wrong meaning, and this file turns it into
    "nothing to carry" — a positive assertion of health about a box several
    releases behind. So the tree is pulled before it is asked.

    **`--ff-only`, and the refusals are the feature.** A diverged branch, a local
    commit, a modified file the fast-forward would overwrite: each of those ends
    here as a sentence, with the tree exactly as it was found. The commands that
    would resolve them instead — `pull --rebase`, `reset --hard`, `checkout --`,
    `stash` — are the ones the harness refuses outright in a shared tree, for the
    reason they are refused there: they destroy uncommitted work, silently, and
    they have done it on this fleet five times. Deciding what happens to somebody's
    divergence is not this file's business at any level of confidence.

    A branch that tracks nothing is not a failure and must not read as one. There is
    nothing to pull it up to, so it is reported and stepped over with `blocked` left
    False — a caller's only use for this is deciding whether it is still entitled to
    say "nothing to carry", and a signal that fires on every worktree on the fleet is
    one that gets ignored inside a week. A **detached HEAD** is the opposite case and
    is separated out above: it looks the same to `@{u}` and means the one thing this
    is looking for.

    **`@{u}` is taken at its word, and that is an assumption.** A checkout on a branch
    tracking `origin/release-old` fast-forwards happily and is still behind whatever
    landed on `main`. Nothing here second-guesses that: which branch a checkout is on
    is a decision somebody made, and a tool that overrode it would pull a worktree onto
    a branch its owner is not working on. What this rules out is a checkout that is
    behind *the thing it is tracking*, which is the state the fleet actually gets into.
    """
    got = Pulled(path=str(where))
    rc, _, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(where), "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "HEAD"])
    if rc != 0:
        # Separated from "no upstream" below, because the two look alike and mean
        # opposite things. A branch that tracks nothing cannot be behind anything.
        # A DETACHED HEAD can be sitting on a commit from three weeks ago, which is
        # precisely the staleness this pull exists to rule out, so it is doubt.
        got.why = (f"{where} is a detached HEAD, so there is no branch to pull — and a "
                   "detached checkout can be sitting on an old commit, which is the state "
                   "this pull exists to rule out")
        got.blocked = True
        return got
    rc, upstream, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(where), "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref",
                           "--symbolic-full-name", "@{u}"])
    if rc != 0 or not upstream:
        got.why = (f"{where} is on a branch that tracks nothing, so there is nothing to "
                   "pull it up to")
        return got
    got.upstream = upstream
    got.before = head_of(where)
    rc, _, err = run(["git", "-C", str(where), "fetch", "--quiet"], timeout=timeout)
    if rc != 0:
        got.why = f"`git fetch` in {where} failed: {tail(err, 3) or f'exit {rc}'}"
        got.blocked = True
        return got
    rc, _, err = run(["git", "-C", str(where), "merge", "--ff-only", upstream])
    if rc != 0:
        got.why = (f"{where} will not fast-forward onto {upstream}: "
                   f"{tail(err, 3) or f'exit {rc}'}. Nothing was merged, rebased or reset — "
                   "a diverged branch or a conflicting local edit is a decision, and it is "
                   "yours to make")
        got.blocked = True
        return got
    got.after = head_of(where)
    return got


def pull_note(got: Pulled | None, what: str) -> str:
    """One line about `got`, for the report a person reads.

    Labelled `pulled` rather than by which tree it is, because the rows below it are
    already `checkout` and `consumer` and a report with two differently-meaning rows
    under one label is a report that gets misread once and distrusted after that.
    """
    if got is None:
        return f"{'pulled':<11}{what}: not pulled (--no-pull)"
    if got.moved:
        return f"{'pulled':<11}{what}: {got.before[:12]} -> {got.after[:12]} ({got.upstream})"
    if got.why:
        return f"{'pulled':<11}{what}: NOT pulled — {got.why}"
    return f"{'pulled':<11}{what}: already level with {got.upstream}"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 1. the checkout the comparison is ABOUT — resolved, and always named
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

@dataclass
class Checkout:
    """The quarterback checkout the harness on PATH is measured against."""

    path: Path
    found_by: str     #: how it was arrived at, so the report can say


def quarterback_checkout(where: Path) -> Path | None:
    """`where`'s repository root if that root carries a `harness/bin`, else None.

    `harness/bin` and not the origin remote, because `harness/bin` is literally
    the thing being compared: it is the directory `qb-doctor` diffs the installed
    harness against, and a repository without one has nothing to say about
    whether this machine's harness is stale. (The origin is checked too, one step
    later, by `repo_slug` — a checkout with no origin cannot be matched to a
    consumer's lock entry either. Two different requirements, both real.)
    """
    rc, top, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(where), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if rc != 0 or not top:
        return None
    root = Path(top)
    return root if (root / "harness" / "bin").is_dir() else None


def resolve_repo(repo_arg: str | None, cfg: dict[str, str]) -> tuple[Checkout | None, str]:
    """``(Checkout, "")`` or ``(None, why-there-is-none)`` — named, cwd, declared, refuse.

    **The rule, stated because its absence is what #414 was.** This used to be
    `Path(args.repo) if args.repo else Path.cwd()` and nothing else, which is a
    resolution rule you can only discover by being burned by it. Run from
    `~/source/nix-fleet` on a box whose harness was 74 commits and eleven releases
    behind, `qb-bump` compared the installed harness against a directory with no
    `harness/` in it at all: `qb-doctor` fell back to the tree beside itself, which
    for an *installed* `qb-doctor` IS the installed harness, and an installed
    harness is identical to itself forever. `ok`. So `qb-bump` printed **"nothing
    to carry"** about a machine that `qb-doctor`, pointed at a real checkout one
    minute later, called `FAIL — 10 differ`.

    So the order is:

    1. `--repo`, which is somebody saying it. A named directory that is not a
       checkout is a typo and is refused rather than fallen back from, the way
       `named_consumer` refuses a `--flake` that pins nothing.
    2. the working directory, when it is inside one — an agent runs this from the
       checkout it is working in, and a person standing in one means that one.
    3. `QUARTERBACK_REPO`, from the environment then the site config. This is the
       door that makes the tool work from anywhere, and it is not a new one: it is
       the same key `qb-env` writes and `qb-mcp` and `qb-doctor` already read to
       find this repo. A fleet that declares itself once gets a `qb-bump` that
       answers correctly from `~`, from `/etc/nixos`, from a consumer's checkout.
    4. nothing. Which is `unknown`, exit 1, "cannot tell" — never exit 0.

    **The refusal is the point of the change.** "Nothing to carry" is a positive
    assertion of health, not silence: a person who reads it stops looking, which
    is exactly what happened. `qb-reconcile` draws this line as could-not-check
    versus nothing-to-report and `qb-doctor`'s rows draw it as `unknown` versus
    `ok`; this file already had the vocabulary in its exit codes (1 is "cannot
    tell") and simply was not reaching for it here.
    """
    if repo_arg:
        named = Path(repo_arg).expanduser()
        root = quarterback_checkout(named)
        if root is None:
            return None, (f"--repo names {named}, which is not a quarterback checkout — no "
                          "harness/bin in it (or it is not a git repository), so there is "
                          "nothing there to compare the harness on PATH against")
        return Checkout(root, "named by --repo"), ""

    cwd = Path.cwd()
    root = quarterback_checkout(cwd)
    if root is not None:
        return Checkout(root, "the working directory"), ""

    declared = os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_REPO") or cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_REPO")
    if declared:
        where = as_path(declared)
        root = quarterback_checkout(where) if where else None
        if root is None:
            return None, (f"cannot tell: QUARTERBACK_REPO names {declared}, which is not a "
                          "quarterback checkout — no harness/bin in it — and the working "
                          f"directory ({cwd}) is not one either, so there is nothing here to "
                          "compare the harness on PATH against. Run it from a checkout or "
                          "pass --repo DIR")
        return Checkout(root, "declared by QUARTERBACK_REPO"), ""

    return None, (f"cannot tell: {cwd} is not a quarterback checkout, and nothing declares "
                  "one, so there is no harness/bin here to compare the harness on PATH "
                  "against. This is NOT 'nothing to carry' — it is not knowing. Run it from "
                  "a quarterback checkout, pass --repo DIR, or set QUARTERBACK_REPO (in the "
                  "environment or ~/.config/quarterback/config) so it works from anywhere")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 2. the drift — asked of qb-doctor, never worked out here
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

@dataclass
class Drift:
    """What `qb-doctor`'s `harness` row says, and nothing this file decided."""

    verdict: str                       #: ok | warn | fail | unknown
    detail: str
    subject: str = ""
    missing: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    differ: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)

    @property
    def stale(self) -> bool:
        return self.verdict == "fail"

    @property
    def current(self) -> bool:
        """`ok` and only `ok`, which is not the same as "not `fail`".

        Found by Codex on #414's own branch, one function away from the bug being
        fixed there and the identical category error: `not stale` used to mean
        "nothing to carry", so a `harness` row of `unknown` — "no harness on PATH
        (create-worktree not found), so nothing to compare this checkout against",
        which is a machine with NO harness on it at all — came back as exit 0 and
        an all-clear. A box that has never had the harness installed is the most
        carrying-needed state there is.
        """
        return self.verdict == "ok"


def doctor_path(script: str) -> str | None:
    """The `qb-doctor` to ask: the one beside this script, else the one on PATH.

    Beside first, and it matters on the branch that adds a check: a checkout's
    `qb-doctor` knows about drift the installed one has never heard of, and the
    installed one is by definition the stale copy this whole file exists because
    of. Falling back to PATH covers the reverse case — this script run out of a
    checkout on a host whose harness ships a `qb-doctor` and whose checkout, for
    whatever reason, does not.
    """
    beside = Path(os.path.realpath(script)).parent / "qb-doctor"
    if beside.is_file():
        return str(beside)
    return shutil.which("qb-doctor")


def harness_drift(repo: Path, script: str) -> tuple[Drift | None, str]:
    """``(Drift, "")`` or ``(None, why-there-is-none)``, from `qb-doctor --json`.

    The verdict is READ, not re-derived. `--only harness` so the board, the
    client, systemd and the network are not consulted to answer a question about
    files on a disk — a doctor row that needs a token would make this refuse to
    work on a box with no board, and staleness is not a board question.
    """
    doctor = doctor_path(script)
    if doctor is None:
        return None, ("no qb-doctor beside this script or on PATH, and the drift is its "
                      "answer to give (#204)")
    rc, out, err = run([sys.executable, doctor, "--json", "--only", "harness",
                        "--repo", str(repo)], timeout=120)
    if rc == 127 or (not out and rc not in (0, 1, 2)):
        return None, f"qb-doctor could not be run: {err or rc}"
    try:
        checks = json.loads(out)["checks"]
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return None, "qb-doctor did not answer with a report this can read"
    row = next((c for c in checks if c.get("name") == "harness"), None)
    if row is None:
        return None, "qb-doctor's report carried no harness row"
    extra = row.get("extra") or {}
    return Drift(verdict=row.get("verdict", "unknown"), detail=row.get("detail", ""),
                 subject=row.get("subject", ""),
                 missing=list(extra.get("missing") or []),
                 differ=list(extra.get("differ") or [])), ""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 3. the consumer — the flake that PINS this repo, which is not this repo
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

@dataclass
class Consumer:
    """A flake that pins this repo: where it is, what it calls the input, what it pins."""

    flake: Path
    input: str        #: what `nix flake update` is given — the root's name for it
    node: str         #: what the lock stores it under, which is not always the name
    rev: str
    found_by: str


def repo_slug(repo: Path) -> str | None:
    """`owner/name` from the checkout's origin, which is what a lock entry names."""
    rc, out, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(repo), "remote", "get-url", "origin"])
    if rc != 0 or not out:
        return None
    m = re.search(r"[:/]([^/:]+)/([^/]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$", out)
    return f"{m.group(1)}/{m.group(2)}" if m else None


def pins_repo(lock_text: str, slug: str) -> tuple[str, str, str] | None:
    """``(input name, node id, locked rev)`` if this lock pins `slug` DIRECTLY, else None.

    Two distinctions Codex made on the first cut of this file, and both of them
    are the difference between updating the right thing and updating something
    else:

    **Direct inputs only.** `nodes` is the whole graph — every transitive
    dependency of every input is in there — so scanning it finds a repository
    that some other flake pins, in a consumer that does not pin it at all.
    `nix flake update` cannot move such a node, and a lock that mentions this
    repo three levels down is not a consumer of this harness. So the walk starts
    at the root node's own `inputs`.

    **The input's NAME is not the node's ID.** A lock disambiguates a second node
    for the same flake as `quarterback_2`, and `nix flake update quarterback_2`
    is not a command: the name to pass is the one the root node uses. Both are
    returned, because the name is what updates and the id is what the lock is
    read by.

    Matched on the LOCKED half rather than the original: an input written as
    `github:owner/repo` and one written as a `git+https` URL to the same
    repository are the same pin, and the locked node is where both spell it out.
    """
    try:
        doc = json.loads(lock_text)
        nodes = doc.get("nodes") or {}
        root = nodes.get(doc.get("root") or "root") or {}
        direct = root.get("inputs") or {}
    except (ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None
    owner, _, name = slug.partition("/")
    for input_name, node_id in direct.items():
        # A `follows` is spelled as a LIST (a path through the graph) rather than
        # a node id. Such an input is somebody else's node by definition, so it is
        # not the input a bump of this repo would move.
        if not isinstance(node_id, str):
            continue
        node = nodes.get(node_id)
        locked = node.get("locked") if isinstance(node, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(locked, dict):
            continue
        same = (str(locked.get("owner", "")).lower() == owner.lower()
                and str(locked.get("repo", "")).lower() == name.lower())
        if not same:
            url = str(locked.get("url", "")).lower()
            same = bool(url) and slug.lower() in url
        if same:
            return str(input_name), node_id, str(locked.get("rev", ""))
    return None


def main_worktree(path: Path) -> Path:
    """The main checkout of whatever repository `path` is a worktree of.

    Eight directories under `~/source` pinned this repo on the machine this was
    written for, and they were eight worktrees of ONE consuming flake — a scan
    that reported eight candidates and refused was correct and useless. They are
    not eight consumers: a machine is rebuilt from the checkout, not from a
    feature branch's worktree, and proposing a bump into one of those would put
    somebody's in-flight branch in the path of their next rebuild.

    Every worktree of a repository shares its common git dir, which is the main
    checkout's `.git`, so its parent is the main checkout. Anything that is not a
    git repository at all — a plain directory with a `flake.lock` in it — is left
    exactly as it was found.
    """
    rc, out, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(path), "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                      "--git-common-dir"])
    if rc == 0 and out and Path(out).name == ".git" and Path(out).parent.is_dir():
        return Path(out).parent
    return path


def scan_roots(roots: list[Path], slug: str) -> list[Consumer]:
    """Every flake under `roots` (one level down, plus the root itself) that pins `slug`.

    One level and no deeper, deliberately. This runs on a laptop with a home
    directory in it, and a recursive walk looking for `flake.lock` is a tool that
    takes a minute to answer a question about a file it already knows the shape
    of. A host that keeps its flake somewhere else says so — that is what
    `QUARTERBACK_CONSUMER_ROOTS` and `--flake` are for.

    Each hit is collapsed onto its main checkout before being counted, so N
    worktrees of one flake are one candidate and not N.
    """
    found: list[Consumer] = []
    seen: set[Path] = set()
    for root in roots:
        if not root.is_dir():
            continue
        candidates = [root / "flake.lock"]
        try:
            candidates += sorted(root.glob("*/flake.lock"))
        except OSError:
            pass
        for lock in candidates:
            if not lock.is_file():
                continue
            flake = main_worktree(lock.parent.resolve()).resolve()
            if flake in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(flake)
            try:
                text = (flake / "flake.lock").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
            except OSError:
                continue
            hit = pins_repo(text, slug)
            if hit:
                found.append(Consumer(flake=flake, input=hit[0], node=hit[1], rev=hit[2],
                                      found_by=f"found by scanning {root}"))
    return found


def consumer_roots(cfg: dict[str, str]) -> list[Path]:
    raw = os.environ.get("QUARTERBACK_CONSUMER_ROOTS") or cfg.get("QUARTERBACK_CONSUMER_ROOTS")
    spec = raw.split(":") if raw else list(DEFAULT_ROOTS)
    return [p for p in (as_path(s) for s in spec if s.strip()) if p]


def named_consumer(where: Path, slug: str, how: str) -> tuple[Consumer | None, str]:
    """A flake somebody NAMED — checked, because a name that pins nothing is a typo."""
    lock = where / "flake.lock"
    try:
        text = lock.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return None, f"{how} names {where}, which has no readable flake.lock"
    hit = pins_repo(text, slug)
    if not hit:
        return None, (f"{how} names {where}, whose flake.lock does not pin {slug} — "
                      "so bumping it would not carry this harness anywhere")
    return Consumer(flake=where.resolve(), input=hit[0], node=hit[1], rev=hit[2],
                    found_by=how), ""


def resolve_consumer(flake_arg: str | None, cfg: dict[str, str],
                     slug: str) -> tuple[Consumer | None, str]:
    """The consuming flake, by the four doors, in order. Ambiguity REFUSES.

    Two candidates is the case worth being careful about: a fleet flake and a
    scratch copy of it both pin this repo, and picking the alphabetically first
    would prepare a bump against a directory nobody rebuilds from — a proposal
    that looks exactly like a good one and does nothing. So it names both and
    stops, which is a question a person answers once with `--flake` or a config
    line.
    """
    if flake_arg:
        return named_consumer(as_path(flake_arg), slug, "--flake")
    env = os.environ.get(FLAKE_KEY)
    if env:
        return named_consumer(as_path(env), slug, f"${FLAKE_KEY}")
    declared = cfg.get(FLAKE_KEY)
    if declared:
        return named_consumer(as_path(declared), slug, f"{FLAKE_KEY} in the site config")
    found = scan_roots(consumer_roots(cfg), slug)
    if not found:
        return None, (f"no flake pinning {slug} under "
                      f"{', '.join(str(r) for r in consumer_roots(cfg))} — name it with "
                      f"--flake, or set {FLAKE_KEY} (programs.quarterback-harness."
                      "consumer.flake)")
    if len(found) > 1:
        names = ", ".join(str(c.flake) for c in found)
        return None, (f"{len(found)} flakes pin {slug} ({names}) — this will not guess "
                      f"which one this machine is rebuilt from; name it with --flake or "
                      f"{FLAKE_KEY}")
    return found[0], ""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 4. the attribute — which nixosConfiguration IS this machine
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def host_attrs(flake: Path) -> list[str]:
    """`nixosConfigurations`' attribute names. Cheap: names do not evaluate configs."""
    rc, out, _ = nix("eval", f"path:{flake}#nixosConfigurations",
                     "--apply", "builtins.attrNames", "--json", timeout=120)
    if rc != 0:
        return []
    try:
        return [str(a) for a in json.loads(out)]
    except ValueError:
        return []


def resolve_attr(flake: Path, attr_arg: str | None, cfg: dict[str, str]) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """``(attr, "")`` or ``("", why-not)`` — which system output is this machine.

    **The attribute is not the hostname**, and assuming it is fails silently in
    the worst possible way: this fleet's `zeus` is `nixosConfigurations.desktop`,
    so a tool that built `#zeus` would report "no such output" on the one machine
    it was written for, and a tool that built the wrong host's `#hermes` would
    hand a person a command that switches their desktop to a laptop's config. So
    the hostname is only a first guess, checked against the flake's own names, and
    the fallback is to ask each configuration what hostname it declares.

    That fallback evaluates configurations, which is slow and can fail — an
    installer image or a VM variant may not evaluate on this machine at all. A
    failure to evaluate is skipped rather than fatal: it means "not this one" for
    the purposes of a question about which host we are.
    """
    if attr_arg:
        return attr_arg, ""
    declared = os.environ.get(ATTR_KEY) or cfg.get(ATTR_KEY)
    if declared:
        return declared, ""
    me = socket.gethostname().split(".", 1)[0]
    attrs = host_attrs(flake)
    if not attrs:
        return "", (f"{flake} declares no nixosConfigurations this can read — name the "
                    f"output with --host or {ATTR_KEY}")
    if me in attrs:
        return me, ""
    matches = []
    for attr in attrs:
        rc, out, _ = nix("eval", f"path:{flake}#nixosConfigurations.{attr}"
                                 ".config.networking.hostName", "--raw", timeout=300)
        if rc == 0 and out == me:
            matches.append(attr)
    if len(matches) == 1:
        return matches[0], ""
    if not matches:
        return "", (f"none of {flake}'s {len(attrs)} nixosConfigurations declares "
                    f"hostName {me!r} — name this machine's output with --host or "
                    f"{ATTR_KEY}")
    return "", (f"{len(matches)} of {flake}'s outputs declare hostName {me!r} "
                f"({', '.join(matches)}) — name the one to build with --host, or once and "
                f"for all with {ATTR_KEY} (programs.quarterback-harness.consumer.attr)")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 5. preparation — on a copy of HEAD, and never on the consumer's working tree
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

@dataclass
class Proposal:
    """A bump that has been prepared AND built, saved for the person who applies it."""

    flake: str
    attr: str
    input: str
    old_rev: str
    new_rev: str
    out_path: str
    base_lock_sha: str
    new_lock_sha: str
    prepared: float
    prepared_by: str
    drift: dict
    #: The consumer's HEAD when this was built. What was proven is THAT commit
    #: plus one moved input, so a consumer who commits afterwards has a system
    #: nothing here has built, and `--apply` refuses rather than switch onto it.
    base_head: str = ""
    #: True when `--host` named the attribute rather than it being matched against
    #: this machine's hostname. It is the wrapper question: a `rebuild` wrapper picks
    #: its attribute from the live hostname, so it agrees with a MATCHED attr by
    #: construction and agrees with a NAMED one only by luck.
    attr_named: bool = False
    #: Tracked files that were modified in the consumer when this was built,
    #: `flake.lock` aside. They were NOT part of what was built — preparation
    #: reads HEAD — but `nixos-rebuild --flake <dir>` builds the working tree, so
    #: a switch WILL include them. Recorded so the person switching is told.
    dirty: list = field(default_factory=list)

    @property
    def moved(self) -> bool:
        return self.old_rev != self.new_rev


def export_head(flake: Path, into: Path) -> str:
    """Unpack `git archive HEAD` of `flake` into `into`. ``""`` on success.

    HEAD and not the working tree, and this is the safety property the whole file
    rests on: the consumer's uncommitted work — a half-written module, a secret
    being edited — can neither be built here nor swept into anything. What gets
    proposed is "the committed consumer, with one input moved", which is the only
    change this tool is entitled to have an opinion about.
    """
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(flake), "archive", "--format=tar", "HEAD"],
                       capture_output=True, timeout=120)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return f"git archive failed in {flake}: {p.stderr.decode(errors='replace').strip()}"
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=io.BytesIO(p.stdout), mode="r|") as tar:
        tar.extractall(into, filter="data")
    return ""


def head_of(flake: Path) -> str:
    rc, out, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(flake), "rev-parse", "HEAD"])
    return out if rc == 0 else ""


def dirty_tracked(flake: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Tracked files modified in the consumer, `flake.lock` aside.

    `flake.lock` is excluded because it is the one file this change is ABOUT and
    :func:`lock_is_committed` has already refused if it was dirty for any other
    reason.

    `diff --name-only` rather than `status --porcelain`: the porcelain format puts
    two status characters before the path, and this file's `run` strips the output
    — which ate the leading space of the first line and with it the first letter of
    the first filename. Names, not statuses, is what a caller wants anyway.
    """
    rc, out, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(flake), "diff", "--name-only", "HEAD"])
    if rc != 0:
        return []
    return [n for n in (line.strip() for line in out.splitlines()) if n and n != "flake.lock"]


def lock_is_committed(flake: Path) -> str:
    """``""`` if the consumer's `flake.lock` is what HEAD says, else why that matters.

    The preparation builds `HEAD` plus one moved input, and `--apply` writes that
    result over the file in the working tree. If those two locks already differ,
    the consumer has an uncommitted lock change of their own — a nixpkgs bump they
    were part-way through — and applying would silently discard it. Reading it
    instead is not the alternative: this file's whole safety property is that it
    does not build somebody's uncommitted work.

    So it stops and says so, which costs a `git commit` or a `git checkout` and is
    the only outcome that neither loses their change nor builds a tree nobody
    wrote.
    """
    rc, _, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(flake), "diff", "--quiet", "HEAD", "--", "flake.lock"])
    if rc == 0:
        return ""
    return (f"{flake}/flake.lock has uncommitted changes. This prepares against HEAD, so "
            "applying the result would discard them — commit or revert that file first")


def prepare(consumer: Consumer, attr: str, script: str, drift: Drift,
            attr_named: bool = False) -> tuple[Proposal | None, str, str]:
    """Update the pin, build the system, and keep what a person needs to switch.

    Returns ``(proposal, build log, why-there-is-none)``. A build that fails
    returns no proposal AND its log, because the log is the whole value of the
    refusal — "it does not build" without the error is a dead end, and this is
    exactly where the `home.file` collision of 2026-08-22 showed up.

    `path:` on every flakeref, and it is not decoration. Given a bare directory
    nix walks UPWARDS looking for a git repository to treat as the flake's source,
    and on a machine where that walk finds something unexpected the build fails
    with `error: opening Git repository "/tmp"` — an error about the temporary
    directory this file chose, reported as if it were about the consumer. `path:`
    says "this directory, as it is", which is what has just been unpacked.
    """
    tmp = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="qb-bump-"))
    try:
        # Read BEFORE the archive, not after the build. `base_head` is the guard
        # `--apply` uses to refuse a consumer that has committed since — and recorded
        # afterwards it recorded whatever the consumer had become during an hour-long
        # build, blessing a commit that was never archived and never built.
        base_head = head_of(consumer.flake)
        base_dirty = dirty_tracked(consumer.flake)
        base_lock = (consumer.flake / "flake.lock").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        why = export_head(consumer.flake, tmp)
        if why:
            return None, "", why
        rc, out, err = nix("flake", "update", consumer.input, "--flake", f"path:{tmp}",
                           timeout=UPDATE_TIMEOUT)
        if rc != 0:
            return None, "", f"`nix flake update {consumer.input}` failed: {tail(err, 8)}"
        new_lock = (tmp / "flake.lock").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        try:
            new_rev = str(json.loads(new_lock)["nodes"][consumer.node]["locked"]["rev"])
        except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
            return None, "", "the updated lock has no rev for the input that was updated"
        rc, out, err = nix("build", f"path:{tmp}#nixosConfigurations.{attr}"
                                    ".config.system.build.toplevel",
                           "--no-link", "--print-out-paths", timeout=BUILD_TIMEOUT)
        log = err or out
        if rc != 0:
            save_refusal(new_rev, log)
            return None, log, (f"the bumped flake does not build "
                               f"(nix build exited {rc}) — not proposing it")
        moved_under_us = head_of(consumer.flake)
        if moved_under_us and base_head and moved_under_us != base_head:
            # Somebody committed in the consumer while this was building. What was
            # proven is the tree as it was an hour ago; recording the new HEAD would
            # make `--apply`'s own guard wave it through.
            return None, log, (f"{consumer.flake} committed {base_head[:12]} -> "
                               f"{moved_under_us[:12]} while this was building, so what "
                               "was built is not what a switch would build. Re-run it.")
        proposal = Proposal(
            base_head=base_head, dirty=base_dirty, attr_named=attr_named,
            flake=str(consumer.flake), attr=attr, input=consumer.input,
            old_rev=consumer.rev, new_rev=new_rev, out_path=out.splitlines()[-1] if out else "",
            base_lock_sha=sha(base_lock), new_lock_sha=sha(new_lock),
            prepared=time.time(), prepared_by=invocation(script),
            drift={"detail": drift.detail, "missing": drift.missing, "differ": drift.differ})
        save(proposal, new_lock, log)
        return proposal, log, ""
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)


def save(proposal: Proposal, lock_text: str, log: str) -> None:
    """Write the proposal, the lock it proposes and the build's output to the cache."""
    CACHE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (CACHE / "flake.lock").write_text(lock_text, encoding="utf-8")
    (CACHE / "build.log").write_text(log, encoding="utf-8")
    (CACHE / "proposal.json").write_text(json.dumps(asdict(proposal), indent=2),
                                         encoding="utf-8")


def save_refusal(rev: str, log: str) -> None:
    """Record that a bump to `rev` was refused, beside whatever is cached.

    The cache is not cleared, and that is the decision rather than an oversight.
    An earlier proposal that DID build is still worth applying — it carries the
    machine most of the way and it was proven — so throwing it away would answer a
    refusal by leaving the person with nothing. What must not happen is applying
    it silently after being told today's bump is broken, which is why `--apply`
    reads this file and says so first.
    """
    try:
        CACHE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        (CACHE / "refused.json").write_text(
            json.dumps({"rev": rev, "at": time.time(), "log": tail(log, 8)}, indent=2),
            encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass


def load_refusal() -> dict | None:
    try:
        got = json.loads((CACHE / "refused.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        return got if isinstance(got, dict) else None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def load() -> Proposal | None:
    try:
        raw = json.loads((CACHE / "proposal.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        return Proposal(**raw)
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError):
        return None


def invocation(script: str) -> str:
    """How to type this command again — the name if it is on PATH, else the full path.

    Bootstrapping, and it is not hypothetical: the harness that carries `qb-bump`
    is by definition the one that is NOT installed on the host it is diagnosing,
    so on the first run there is no `qb-bump` on PATH and a report that says
    `qb-bump --apply` gives the reader a `command not found`. When the name
    resolves to this very file it is used, because a short command is easier to
    trust; otherwise the report prints the path that just worked.
    """
    me = Path(os.path.realpath(script))
    on_path = shutil.which(me.name)
    if on_path and Path(os.path.realpath(on_path)) == me:
        return me.name
    return str(me)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 6. the escalation — through #274's door, in #279's vocabulary
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def loops_dir(script: str) -> Path | None:
    """`harness/loops`, in the two layouts there are — a checkout and the package.

    The same two-place lookup `qb-hooks` does for `githooks/`, and for the same
    reason: `bin/` and `share/quarterback-harness/loops` are siblings in the store
    output, and `bin/` and `loops/` are siblings in a checkout.
    """
    here = Path(os.path.realpath(script)).parent
    for cand in (os.environ.get("QB_LOOPS_DIR"),
                 here.parent / "loops",
                 here.parent / "share" / "quarterback-harness" / "loops"):
        if cand and Path(cand, "needs_human.py").is_file():
            return Path(cand)
    return None


def escalate(script: str, *, summary: str, reason: str, detail: str, repo: str,
             key_parts: list[str]) -> str:
    """Tell the board a human has to finish this — through `needs_human.announce`.

    Not a `board_post` written here, and not a print. #274 landed one door for
    "a human must do this" and #279 the vocabulary it speaks; a second spelling of
    the same escalation is how a fleet ends up with two places to look and a
    person watching neither. The class is `environment`: nothing about this needs
    taste or a design decision, it needs root on a particular machine.

    Never raises and never blocks. An unannounced escalation is still an
    escalation — it is printed either way, and the return value says which.
    """
    where = loops_dir(script)
    if where is None:
        return ("needs-human NOT announced: no harness/loops beside this script, so "
                "#274's door is not reachable from here")
    sys.path.insert(0, str(where))
    try:
        import needs_human  # noqa: PLC0415 — resolved at call time, by design
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — an escalation must survive its own courier
        return f"needs-human NOT announced: {type(exc).__name__} importing needs_human"
    return needs_human.announce(
        cls=NEEDS_HUMAN_CLASS, reason=reason, summary=summary, detail=detail, repo=repo,
        key=needs_human.digest(*key_parts),
        refs=[{"kind": "issue", "value": "267", "repo": repo}])


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 7. --apply — the whole job, run by the person who has the sudo
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def wrapper_targets(script: Path) -> tuple[set[str], str]:
    """Every flake directory `script` names, read out of its own text.

    **Read rather than run.** A `rebuild` wrapper is a shell script that ends in
    `nixos-rebuild switch --flake <somewhere>#<attr>` and nothing can ask it which
    `<somewhere>` — there is no `--print-target`. Finding out by running it under a
    shimmed `nixos-rebuild` would mean EXECUTING an arbitrary script on an arbitrary
    host in order to answer a question about it, which is a worse trade than reading
    it: the read cannot do anything, and its failure mode is falling back to a
    command that was already correct.

    **Read rather than trust.** The flake this file resolved and built is not
    necessarily the one a wrapper targets — a host can have several, and switching
    onto a system nobody here built is the single outcome the whole file is arranged
    to prevent. So the answer is used only when the scan finds exactly one directory
    and it is that flake. No match, two matches, or a flakeref assembled out of shell
    variables this cannot evaluate all mean "cannot show it", and "cannot show it"
    means the explicit command.

    **And it is a heuristic, which is said here rather than left to be discovered.**
    A regex is not a shell parser. Commented-out lines are dropped for that reason —
    a `# flake=path:/the/real/one` above a live `--flake "path:$other#rescue"` was the
    exact decoy that made this necessary — but a target assembled from variables can
    still hide from it, and a wrapper whose text disagrees with its behaviour will be
    trusted. Three things bound the damage: the flakeref has to be *this* flake and
    the only one in the file, `--host` refuses the wrapper outright (see
    :func:`rebuild_wrapper`), and `--no-wrapper` turns the whole path off. What is
    NOT claimed is proof.
    """
    try:
        raw = script.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    except OSError as exc:
        return set(), f"the `rebuild` on PATH ({script}) cannot be read: {exc}"
    # Whole-line comments only. A trailing `#` is left alone deliberately: it is how
    # every flakeref in a wrapper is written (`path:/dir#${host}`), and stripping from
    # the first `#` could delete a second, real target and turn "two directories, so
    # cannot tell" into "one directory, so trusted" — the wrong direction.
    text = "\n".join("" if ln.lstrip().startswith("#") else ln
                     for ln in raw.splitlines())
    found = {os.path.realpath(m.group(1)) for m in re.finditer(
        r"""(?:--flake[= ]|\bflake=)["']?(?:path:)?(/[^\s"'#$]+)""", text)}
    # And every bare `path:/…` too, whatever variable it is being assigned to. A wrapper
    # that sets `target="path:/other"` and passes `--flake "$target"` later would slip past
    # the pattern above, leaving one unrelated `flake=` looking like agreement. This can
    # only ever widen the set, and a wider set falls back to the explicit command — the
    # safe direction for a check whose entire job is being sure.
    found |= {os.path.realpath(m.group(1))
              for m in re.finditer(r"""\bpath:(/[^\s"'#$]+)""", text)}
    if not found:
        return set(), (f"the `rebuild` on PATH ({script}) names no flake directory this can "
                       "read, so there is no way to show that it builds this one")
    return found, ""


def rebuild_wrapper(proposal: Proposal,
                    cfg: dict[str, str]) -> tuple[list[str] | None, str, str]:
    """The host's `nixos-rebuild` wrapper, when it can be SHOWN to build what was built.

    ``(argv, why it is being used, what a person would type)`` or ``(None, why it is
    not, "")`` — and the second is not an error, it is the ordinary answer on every
    host that has no wrapper.

    **`argv[0]` is a resolved path and the third value is not.** What runs has to be
    the file that was read, so `argv[0]` is the realpath; what a REPORT says has to be
    something a person recognises, and `/nix/store/…-hm_rebuild switch` is not that.
    Both, rather than one of them wrongly.

    **Why a wrapper at all.** `nixos-rebuild switch` prints "Done. The new
    configuration is …" even when `home-manager-<user>.service` has failed, so
    `home.file` links, user units and dotfiles do not apply and the switch says
    nothing about it. For this file that is not somebody else's bug: the harness
    scripts it exists to deliver arrive through exactly that activation, so a
    bypassed wrapper means #267's own failure — a machine that did not get the
    change — reported as a success. This fleet's `rebuild` surfaces it, and the
    right response to a guard that already exists is to call it, not to grow a
    second copy of it here (the same argument that makes the drift verdict
    `qb-doctor`'s and not this file's).
    """
    declared = os.environ.get(REBUILD_KEY) or cfg.get(REBUILD_KEY)
    if declared:
        try:
            argv = shlex.split(declared)
        except ValueError as exc:
            # An unbalanced quote in a config file must not be a traceback out of a
            # tool whose next act is switching a machine.
            return None, f"{REBUILD_KEY} ({declared!r}) will not parse as an argv: {exc}", ""
        if not argv:
            return None, f"{REBUILD_KEY} is set to an empty command, so it says nothing", ""
        binary = shutil.which(argv[0])
        if not binary:
            return None, f"{REBUILD_KEY} names {argv[0]}, which is not on PATH", ""
        typed = " ".join(argv)
        return [binary, *argv[1:]], f"{REBUILD_KEY} declares `{typed}`", typed

    if proposal.attr_named:
        # A wrapper derives its attribute from the live hostname; `resolve_attr` also
        # matches this machine's hostname unless somebody overrode it. So a MATCHED
        # attr and the wrapper's agree by construction, and a NAMED one — `--host
        # laptop` on a desktop — agrees only by luck. Getting that wrong switches this
        # machine onto a configuration this run never built, which is the whole thing
        # the file is arranged to prevent, and no reading of the wrapper can rule it
        # out because the attribute never appears in the text.
        return None, (f"--host named {proposal.attr!r} rather than it being matched against "
                      "this machine's hostname, and a `rebuild` wrapper picks its own "
                      "attribute from the hostname — so it cannot be shown to build the "
                      "one that was built"), ""

    found = shutil.which("rebuild")
    if not found:
        return None, "no `rebuild` wrapper on PATH", ""
    # The file that gets READ is the file that gets EXEC'd: `shutil.which` and a later
    # `execvp` are two independent PATH lookups, and inspecting one file while running
    # whatever the second lookup turns up is the check quietly reopening itself.
    binary = os.path.realpath(found)
    targets, why = wrapper_targets(Path(binary))
    if why:
        return None, why, ""
    want = os.path.realpath(proposal.flake)
    if targets != {want}:
        return None, (f"the `rebuild` on PATH builds {', '.join(sorted(targets))}, not "
                      f"{want} — so it is not this flake's wrapper"), ""
    return ([binary, "switch"],
            f"`rebuild` ({found}) builds {want}, and it surfaces a failed home-manager "
            "activation that `nixos-rebuild` reports as success",
            "rebuild switch")


def switch_command(proposal: Proposal, cfg: dict[str, str] | None = None,
                   wrapper: bool = True) -> tuple[list[str], str, str]:
    """``(argv, why it is that and not the other, what a person would type)``."""
    explicit = ["sudo", "nixos-rebuild", "switch", "--flake",
                f"{proposal.flake}#{proposal.attr}"]
    if not wrapper:
        return explicit, "--no-wrapper: the explicit command was asked for", " ".join(explicit)
    argv, why, typed = rebuild_wrapper(proposal, cfg or {})
    if argv:
        return argv, why, typed
    return explicit, why, " ".join(explicit)


def apply(proposal: Proposal | None, dry_run: bool, cfg: dict[str, str] | None = None,
          wrapper: bool = True) -> int:
    """Install the prepared lock into the consumer and hand the terminal to `sudo`.

    Every refusal here is about applying something OTHER than what was built. The
    lock is compared against the one that was in place when the proposal was
    prepared: if the consumer's lock has moved since, the build that was proven is
    a build of a tree that no longer exists, and re-preparing costs a few minutes
    against the alternative of switching a machine onto something nobody built.

    **A terminal is required, and that is the point of the whole file.** `sudo`
    with no tty either fails or, worse, succeeds from a cached credential in some
    other session — an agent switching a live machine because a person happened to
    have authenticated ten minutes ago. So this refuses without one and prints the
    command instead, which is the correct outcome for every non-human caller.
    """
    if proposal is None:
        print("qb-bump: no prepared bump in the cache to apply. Run `qb-bump --apply` "
              "without --cached and it will prepare one.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    flake = Path(proposal.flake)
    lock = flake / "flake.lock"
    try:
        current = lock.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as exc:
        print(f"qb-bump: cannot read {lock}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    try:
        fresh = (CACHE / "flake.lock").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        print(f"qb-bump: {CACHE / 'flake.lock'} is gone, so the lock this proposal names "
              "cannot be installed. Re-run `qb-bump`.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    if sha(fresh) != proposal.new_lock_sha:
        # The cached lock is not the one this proposal was built from — a second
        # qb-bump wrote the cache between the build and this call, or a write was
        # interrupted. Installing it would switch onto a lock nothing here built,
        # which is the one thing this file exists to prevent.
        print(f"qb-bump: the cached lock is not the one this proposal was built from "
              "(another run wrote it, or a write was interrupted). Re-run `qb-bump`.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    now = head_of(Path(proposal.flake))
    if proposal.base_head and now and now != proposal.base_head:
        print(f"qb-bump: {proposal.flake} has committed {proposal.base_head[:12]} -> "
              f"{now[:12]} since this bump was built, so the system that was proven is not "
              "the one a switch would build now. Re-run `qb-bump`.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    refusal = load_refusal()
    if refusal and refusal.get("at", 0) > proposal.prepared:
        print(f"note: a later bump (to {str(refusal.get('rev', ''))[:12]}) was refused "
              "because it does not build. This applies the earlier one, which does.")
    already = sha(current) == proposal.new_lock_sha
    if not already and sha(current) != proposal.base_lock_sha:
        print(f"qb-bump: {lock} has changed since this bump was prepared — the build that "
              "was proven is not a build of this tree any more. Re-run `qb-bump`.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    cmd, how, _typed = switch_command(proposal, cfg, wrapper)
    if dry_run:
        print(f"would write {lock} ({proposal.input} -> {proposal.new_rev[:12]})"
              if not already else f"{lock} already carries {proposal.new_rev[:12]}")
        print(f"then, because {how}:")
        print(" ".join(cmd))
        return 0
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        print("qb-bump: --apply needs a terminal, because the next thing it does is ask "
              "for a password. Nothing has been changed. Run this yourself:\n"
              f"  {proposal.prepared_by} --apply", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    if not already:
        # Same directory, then rename: `write_text` truncates first, so a full disk or
        # a signal between the truncate and the write leaves the consumer with an empty
        # `flake.lock` and no way to tell it was this that did it.
        scratch = lock.with_name(f".flake.lock.qb-bump.{os.getpid()}")
        try:
            scratch.write_text(fresh, encoding="utf-8")
            os.replace(scratch, lock)
        except OSError as exc:
            scratch.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            print(f"qb-bump: could not install the prepared lock into {lock}: {exc}. "
                  "Nothing was changed.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        print(f"wrote {lock}: {proposal.input} {proposal.old_rev[:12]} -> "
              f"{proposal.new_rev[:12]}")
        print("(that file is the ONLY thing this wrote; committing it is yours)")
    if proposal.dirty:
        # `nixos-rebuild --flake <dir>` builds the WORKING TREE; this built HEAD.
        # Said rather than refused: an uncommitted module is a normal state for a
        # person's own flake, and refusing here would make the tool useless on the
        # machine it was written for. What it must not do is let somebody believe
        # the switch ahead of them is the one that was proven.
        print(f"note: {len(proposal.dirty)} modified file(s) in {proposal.flake} "
              f"({', '.join(proposal.dirty[:3])}) were NOT part of what was built — the "
              "switch below builds your working tree, so it includes them.")
    print(f"switching, because {how}:")
    print(" ".join(cmd))
    os.execvp(cmd[0], cmd)
    return 0  # unreachable; execvp does not return


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 8. the report
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def render_proposal(proposal: Proposal, drift: Drift, checkout: Checkout,
                    consumer: Consumer, announced: str, pulls: list[str] | None = None,
                    because: str = "", how: str = "", caveat: str = "") -> str:
    n = len(drift.missing) + len(drift.differ)
    lines = [(because or f"stale: {drift.detail}") + caveat, ""]
    lines += pulls or []
    lines += [
        f"harness    {drift.subject or 'the one on PATH'}",
        f"checkout   {checkout.path} ({checkout.found_by})",
        f"consumer   {consumer.flake} ({consumer.found_by})",
        f"input      {proposal.input}",
    ]
    if proposal.moved:
        lines.append(f"pin        {proposal.old_rev[:12]} -> {proposal.new_rev[:12]}")
    else:
        lines.append(f"pin        {proposal.new_rev[:12]} already — this machine has not "
                     "been rebuilt onto its own pin")
    lines += [
        f"built      {proposal.attr}: {proposal.out_path or 'ok'}",
        "",
        # `n` is 0 when the reason to act is the consumer having moved rather than the
        # harness having drifted, and "0 scripts arrive on PATH" is a true sentence that
        # reads as an argument for doing nothing.
        (f"{n} script{'s arrive' if n != 1 else ' arrives'} on PATH when a person runs:" if n else
         "This machine catches up with the flake it is built from when a person runs:"),
        f"  {proposal.prepared_by} --apply",
        "",
        "That writes the prepared flake.lock into the consumer and then switches this "
        f"machine with `{how or 'nixos-rebuild switch'}`, which needs a password; nothing "
        "above it did.",
    ]
    if announced:
        lines += ["", announced]
    return "\n".join(lines)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 9. the run — everything that needs no password, then the one thing that does
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

def prepared_bump(args, cfg: dict[str, str],
                  script: str) -> tuple[dict, int, Proposal | None, str | None]:
    """Pull both trees, compare, move the pin, build it. No `sudo` on any path through here.

    ``(report, exit code, proposal, rendered text)``. Exit **2 and only exit 2**
    carries a proposal; every other code is a reason not to switch, and `--apply`
    propagates it rather than reaching for a password anyway. Splitting this out of
    `main` is what lets `--apply` be one command: the person's path and the agent's
    path now run the identical preparation and diverge only at the end of it.
    """
    out: dict = {"outcome": "", "detail": ""}

    checkout, why = resolve_repo(args.repo, cfg)
    if checkout is None:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown", "detail": why}, 1, None, None
    repo = checkout.path
    out["repo"] = {"path": str(repo), "found_by": checkout.found_by}
    against = f"compared against {repo} ({checkout.found_by})"

    # Before the comparison, never after it: the verdict is *about* this tree.
    pulled = None if args.no_pull else fast_forward(repo)
    pulls: dict = {"checkout": asdict(pulled) if pulled else None}
    out["pull"] = pulls
    notes = [pull_note(pulled, "checkout")]

    drift, why = harness_drift(repo, script)
    if drift is None:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown", "detail": why}, 1, None, None
    out["drift"] = {"verdict": drift.verdict, "detail": drift.detail,
                    "subject": drift.subject, "missing": drift.missing, "differ": drift.differ}
    installed = f"{drift.subject}, " if drift.subject else ""
    if not drift.current and not drift.stale:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown",
                "detail": f"cannot tell: qb-doctor's harness row is {drift.verdict}, not ok "
                          f"— {drift.detail} ({installed}{against})"}, 1, None, None

    # The consumer is resolved for BOTH verdicts, because pulling it can itself be
    # the reason to act: a flake commit landed from another box is a rebuild this
    # machine owes whether or not the harness pin moved. Its refusals are still
    # only *reported* on the path that needs one, in the order they were reported
    # before — a host with no nix must hear about the nix, not about a consumer.
    nix_here, slug = have_nix(), repo_slug(repo)
    consumer: Consumer | None = None
    consumer_why = ""
    consumer_pull: Pulled | None = None
    if nix_here and slug:
        consumer, consumer_why = resolve_consumer(args.flake, cfg, slug)
        if consumer is not None and not args.no_pull:
            consumer_pull = fast_forward(consumer.flake)
            pulls["consumer"] = asdict(consumer_pull)
            notes.append(pull_note(consumer_pull, "consumer"))
            if consumer_pull.moved:
                # The pull moves `flake.lock` too, and with it the rev this bump is
                # FROM. Re-read THAT path — not `resolve_consumer` again: rerunning
                # discovery after the pull can land on a different flake (the pulled one
                # stops pinning this repo, a sibling starts), and then `consumer_pull`
                # and the reason printed would describe one tree while the bump was
                # prepared in another.
                consumer, consumer_why = named_consumer(consumer.flake, slug,
                                                        consumer.found_by)

    # **Which tree's blocked pull is doubt, and which is a footnote.** The verdict is
    # a claim about the CHECKOUT, so a checkout that could not be brought level with its
    # upstream makes the verdict itself unsafe to report either way — that is #414's
    # lesson and it downgrades to `unknown`. A consuming flake that could not be fetched
    # is the same class of thing as one that could not be found: it does not weaken the
    # harness comparison by one word, it only means the SECOND reason to act was never
    # looked for. Downgrading on it would be worse than useless — a consumer whose
    # remote wants a credential no non-interactive session has would make every agent
    # run of this answer "cannot tell" forever, and a tool that always says that is a
    # tool nobody reads. So it is said, in the same sentence, and not hidden.
    if pulled is not None and pulled.blocked:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown",
                "detail": f"cannot tell: {pulled.why} — so neither an all-clear nor a bump "
                          "would be a claim about a checkout that is level with what it "
                          f"tracks ({installed}{against})"}, 1, None, None

    caveat = ""
    if args.no_pull:
        caveat = (" — neither tree was pulled (--no-pull), so this is not a claim about "
                  "what has landed on their upstreams")
    elif consumer_pull is not None and consumer_pull.blocked:
        caveat = (f" — the consuming flake could not be brought up to date "
                  f"({consumer_pull.why}), so anything landed there is not accounted for")
    elif consumer is None:
        unchecked = ("there is no nix on this host" if not nix_here else
                     "this checkout has no origin remote" if not slug else consumer_why)
        caveat = (f" — the consuming flake was not checked ({unchecked}), so this is a "
                  "claim about the harness and not about that flake")

    because = ""
    if drift.current:
        # What "nothing to carry" does NOT cover is said in the same sentence. It is a
        # positive assertion of health that a reader stops looking after, and since the
        # consuming flake having moved is now a reason to act on its own, a run that
        # never got as far as looking at one must not imply that it did.
        if consumer_pull is None or not consumer_pull.moved:
            return {**out, "outcome": "current",
                    "detail": f"nothing to carry: {drift.detail} "
                              f"({installed}{against}){caveat}"}, 0, None, None
        because = (f"the harness on PATH is this checkout's, but {consumer_pull.path} moved "
                   f"{consumer_pull.before[:12]} -> {consumer_pull.after[:12]} on the pull, "
                   "so this machine is behind the flake it is built from")

    if not nix_here:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown",
                "detail": "no nix on this host, and a flake bump is the only thing this "
                          "knows how to prepare"}, 1, None, None
    if not slug:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown",
                "detail": f"{repo} has no origin remote, so nothing here knows which "
                          "repository a consumer would be pinning"}, 1, None, None
    if consumer is None:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown", "detail": consumer_why}, 1, None, None
    out["consumer"] = {"flake": str(consumer.flake), "input": consumer.input,
                       "found_by": consumer.found_by}

    why = lock_is_committed(consumer.flake)
    if why:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown", "detail": why}, 1, None, None

    attr, why = resolve_attr(consumer.flake, args.attr, cfg)
    if not attr:
        return {**out, "outcome": "unknown", "detail": why}, 1, None, None
    out["attr"] = attr

    proposal, log, why = prepare(consumer, attr, script, drift,
                                 attr_named=bool(args.attr))
    if proposal is None:
        announced = "" if args.no_announce else escalate(
            script,
            summary=f"the flake bump for {consumer.flake.name} does not build — "
                    "this machine cannot take today's harness",
            reason=("bumping the quarterback input and building this machine's system fails, "
                    "so proposing the bump would propose breaking the machine; only a person "
                    "can decide what gives"),
            detail=f"{why}\n\n{tail(log)}", repo=slug,
            key_parts=["qb-bump", "build-failed", str(consumer.flake), attr,
                       sha(tail(log, 5))])
        text = "\n".join([why, "", tail(log)] + ([announced] if announced else []))
        return ({**out, "outcome": "refused", "detail": why, "log": tail(log),
                 "announced": announced}, 4, None, text)

    cmd, how, typed = switch_command(proposal, cfg, not args.no_wrapper)
    out["switch"] = {"command": cmd, "how": how, "typed": typed}

    me = socket.gethostname().split(".", 1)[0]
    n = len(drift.missing) + len(drift.differ)
    # No "a human must do this" under --apply: the human is holding the keyboard.
    # An escalation raised at the moment it is being answered is noise on a board
    # somebody else has to read, and #274's door is not a logbook.
    announced = "" if (args.no_announce or args.apply) else escalate(
        script,
        summary=(f"{n} harness scripts are not on {me}: a built bump is waiting"
                 if proposal.moved else
                 f"{n} harness scripts are not on {me}: the pin already names them and "
                 "this machine has not been rebuilt onto it"),
        reason=("a nixos-rebuild switch needs root and no agent has it; the bump is prepared "
                "and has been built, so what is left is one command by a person"),
        detail="\n".join([
            drift.detail,
            f"checkout: {repo} ({checkout.found_by})",
            f"consumer: {consumer.flake} (input {consumer.input})",
            f"pin:      {proposal.old_rev[:12]} -> {proposal.new_rev[:12]}",
            f"built:    {attr} -> {proposal.out_path}",
            "",
            f"run: {proposal.prepared_by} --apply",
        ]), repo=slug,
        key_parts=["qb-bump", "prepared", str(consumer.flake), attr, proposal.new_rev])
    out.update({"outcome": "prepared", "proposal": asdict(proposal), "announced": announced,
                "detail": f"a bump to {proposal.new_rev[:12]} is built and waiting for "
                          f"`{proposal.prepared_by} --apply`"})
    return (out, 2, proposal,
            render_proposal(proposal, drift, checkout, consumer, announced, pulls=notes,
                            because=because, how=typed, caveat=caveat))


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="qb-bump",
        description="Carry a landed harness change onto this machine: pull, bump the "
                    "consuming flake, build it, and — with --apply — switch onto it (#267).")
    parser.add_argument("--repo", metavar="DIR",
                        help="the quarterback checkout the harness is compared against "
                             "(default: the cwd's, then $QUARTERBACK_REPO; with neither, "
                             "this refuses rather than reporting no drift)")
    parser.add_argument("--flake", metavar="DIR",
                        help="the consuming flake (default: $" + FLAKE_KEY + ", the site "
                             "config, then a scan)")
    parser.add_argument("--host", metavar="ATTR", dest="attr",
                        help="the nixosConfigurations output that IS this machine "
                             "(default: matched by hostName)")
    parser.add_argument("--apply", action="store_true",
                        help="the whole job, run by a person: pull, bump, build and switch")
    parser.add_argument("--cached", action="store_true",
                        help="with --apply, switch onto the proposal already in the cache "
                             "and prepare nothing (for a host that has lost its network)")
    parser.add_argument("--no-pull", action="store_true",
                        help="compare and build the two trees exactly as they stand")
    parser.add_argument("--no-wrapper", action="store_true",
                        help="switch with `sudo nixos-rebuild` rather than this host's "
                             "`rebuild` wrapper")
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                        help="with --apply, do everything but the switch and print its "
                             "command")
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", dest="as_json",
                        help="the outcome as a document")
    parser.add_argument("--no-announce", action="store_true",
                        help="do not post the escalation to the board")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    if args.dry_run and not args.apply:
        print("qb-bump: --dry-run is about --apply; preparing a bump changes nothing on "
              "this machine anyway, which is the point of it", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    if args.cached and not args.apply:
        print("qb-bump: --cached is about --apply; there is nothing else it could mean",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 3

    script = __file__
    cfg = site_config()

    if args.cached:
        return apply(load(), args.dry_run, cfg=cfg, wrapper=not args.no_wrapper)

    out, code, proposal, text = prepared_bump(args, cfg, script)
    if not args.apply or code != 2:
        return report(args, out, code, text=text)
    print(text)
    print("")
    return apply(proposal, args.dry_run, cfg=cfg, wrapper=not args.no_wrapper)


def report(args, out: dict, code: int, text: str | None = None) -> int:
    """One place decides stdout, so `--json` is a document and never a document plus prose."""
    if args.as_json:
        print(json.dumps({**out, "exit_code": code}, indent=2))
    else:
        stream = sys.stdout if code in (0, 2) else sys.stderr
        print(text if text is not None else f"qb-bump: {out.get('detail', '')}", file=stream)
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
